Z||000010||Formula88||12-24-2001||09:33 AM||||Actually, copywright laws are a bit stricter than that.  A photographer holds copywright to all of their work.  The only stipulation there is they also have to have permission from anyone in the photo to publish their image, unless it's a "candid" or "public" image that could be viewed by anyone.  <P>i.e., Studio pics of Britney Spears are copyrighted by the photographer if Britney signed the rights over (of course, a star like her would retian the rights and keep the copyright herself - paying the photog for his time, but not giving him any rights or royalties).<BR> <BR>Photos of a bus station with people milling about don't require the people's approval, since it's a public setting that anyone is accessible to.<P>I don't know the exact wording, but essentially, yes, you do have to ask permission.  ||||reg||3
