Z||000014||Patrick's Dad||12-24-2001||02:03 PM||||Copyright laws are even more strict than that. Any original creation is copywritten, with no mark necessary. Any reproduction thereof is a violation without written consent, except for certain (and limited) "fair use" cases, such as a book report or other educational thesis.<P>Any recognizable person must give written release for an image to be used commercially. A newspaper does not need this, but an advertiser does. I have pictures of George Foreman, Al Jarreau and George Benson that are only for my personal collection, partly because I don't have permission to make money off them, but mostly they're for my collection only. In the above example, it depends on who commisioned the shoot:<P>If Britney commissioned the shoot, then her photographer was "for hire," and the rights belong to Britney. She then gave or sold rights to Rolling Stone to publish them.<P>If Rolling Stone commisioned the shoot, the photographer was still "for hire," or possibly an employee. In this case, Britney got paid (she is a contract employee), and Rolling Stone retains rights.<P>If the photographer commissioned the shoot, then s/he owns the rights. S/he paid Britney, and Rolling Stone paid him/her for reproduction rights.<P>Copyright exists as long as the artist, or an agent for that artist, exists. This is why certain activities are shaky propositions at best to endeavor in.<P>Technically, Pontiac owns the copyrights (and the trademarks) to the Fiero, as well as their own name. I imagine, though, that (unlike Carrol Shelby) they feel that shutting us and other like forums, sites and aftermarket co.'s would be a marketing black eye, as long as we behave ourselves and don't besmirtch the Pontiac name.||||reg||1
